many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy  and practice i've created this series to  provide short answers to the most common  recurring or confusing questions about  stoicism  excellent question about stoicism that  gets asked over and over  is is stoicism compatible  with other belief systems and it gets  asked in terms of specific belief  systems quite often but it also gets  asked  as a general question so  in answering this we really need to  think  about three different aspects of this we  want to think  about the question well what do you  actually  mean by compatible we want to  clarify what is meant by  belief systems and we also interestingly  enough  have to be kind of careful about what we  mean  by stoicism in this case and i think  that's really the best  place to begin because  we want to be very clear about the  kind of stoicism that we're asking about  so there are multiple interpretations of  stoicism  out there in the present some of them  will say are more superficial some  are much closer to the core of  classical stoicism and involves some  sort of modern  application or reinterpretation of this  so at the very start we want to  put aside very superficial treatments of  stoicism where people are saying it's  it's a set of life hacks  or here's the five you know principles  of stoicism  or any of that sort of stuff because  that's not actually stoicism that's just  a few  ideas derived from stoicism slapped into  a blog post or somebody's  you know coaching video or something  like that and it's not  it's not the actual philosophy itself so  it's not very helpful to talk about that  we also want to put aside  basically mistaken or even  exploitative notions of stoicism or  interpretations of that  so i'm going to talk about what we  sometimes  call stoicism written with a dollar sign  for the s and that's often quite  superficial but there'll be some sort of  you know systematic thing laid out for  people about how stoicism is going to  make you super productive  and able to make tons and tons of money  and  maybe not even have to sleep well that's  not really stoicism  either and we also want to put aside the  the deformation of philosophy that i  i even hesitate to call stoicism we  actually jokingly call it broicism  because it's it's you know calling a  bunch of quotes about  toughness and manliness and  you know emphasizes the importance of  courage  gets virtue wrong construing it you know  in terms of manliness  because you know it comes from the word  viewer and  that's not actual stoicism either  although there's a huge market out there  for people who are like you know talking  about how their stoics at the gym  and so tough man and and sometimes  talking about it in terms of  um ethnonationalism and you know a lot  of  sort of misogynist stuff as well so  let's put all that stuff aside we're not  going to worry too much about that  at this point if we do that  then we can talk about one big divide  and that is between those who call  themselves traditional stoics and those  the much larger group that call  themselves  modern stoic so traditional stoics would  say that  modern stoics have given in on  too many key stoic doctrines you know  for example  is the universe providentially arranged  according to some  vast cosmic rationality or is that not  the case  traditional stoics would say yes that's  basically almost like an article of  faith  and many modern stoics would say well i  don't know about that or  it doesn't seem to be that way or they  can say that it is  there's a lot more variance within  modern stoicism  and you know i would say that if we want  to call something modern stoicism it has  to be robust it has to be grounded  in classical stoicism but it doesn't  have to necessarily  be bound by everything that epictetus or  seneca  or musonius rufus or marcus aurelius  said  prime example is i do have a beard and  epictetus thinks that that's really you  know  more in accordance with nature for men  to have beards i think we can dispense  with that and  and i don't even think that the  traditional stoics think that we need to  hold on to  things along those lines so that's  that probably the stoicism that we're  talking about and  if you have read a lot of classic stoic  literature  i'm talking about epictetus and seneca  and you know accounts of these people  you actually know that stoicism itself  was not a rigid orthodoxy seneca  actually weighs  in about this and says you know we get  to decide  so long as there's sort of a core there  what we're going to  focus on and you notice that seneca is  perfectly willing to take things  even from the epicureans if he thinks  that they got things right  middle stoicism with posidonius and  peneteus were engaging in some serious  consideration of whether maybe some of  the other uh virtue ethics traditions  like platonism and aristotelianism  were getting things right so you know  it's not as if stoicism ever had a  you must think just these ways kind of  view  but we can say that there's a vast  network of ideas  and you don't want to lose too many of  them so  that said let's talk about compatibility  well what do we mean in saying that one  belief system  is compatible with the other there's a  couple ways we could construe this  one would be to say that there are no  fundamental contradictions between them  so they can fit together sort of like my  fingers are doing here  or like legos that click into each other  whatever it's going to be we might also  think of it  in culinary metaphors you can pair this  drink  with this dish and they're going to work  well together this one over here you  can't you know  like when you you drink orange juice  after just brushing your teeth most  people  find that disagreeable on a gustatory  level that's almost like a  a contradiction that is being registered  so is stoicism going to be compatible  in that sense with some belief systems  um you know 